The whole shape has `2 * n - 1` rows. For each row, the number of stars is found by counting up to `n` and then counting back down. The gap is based on that star count.

```javascript
class Solution {
    pattern20(n) {
        const totalRows = 2 * n - 1;

        for (let row = 1; row <= totalRows; row++) {
            const stars = row <= n ? row : totalRows - row + 1;
            const spaces = 2 * (n - stars);

            const leftStars = "*".repeat(stars);
            const middleGap = " ".repeat(spaces);
            const rightStars = "*".repeat(stars);

            console.log(leftStars + middleGap + rightStars);
        }
    }
}
```

Dry run for `n = 4`: the calculated star counts are `1, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 1`. The matching gap counts are `6, 4, 2, 0, 2, 4, 6`.

There is only one main row loop here. The conditional formula changes direction after the center, so a separate lower-half loop is not required.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the strings created for the current row.

A row can be made in three pieces: stars on the left, spaces in the middle, and stars on the right. Building the full row first makes the two halves easy to keep equal.

The second loop starts at `n - 1`, so the center row is printed only once.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern20(int n) {
        for (int stars = 1; stars <= n; stars++) {
            printRow(stars, 2 * (n - stars));
        }

        for (int stars = n - 1; stars >= 1; stars--) {
            printRow(stars, 2 * (n - stars));
        }
    }

    private void printRow(int stars, int spaces) {
        StringBuilder row = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
            row.append('*');
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
            row.append(' ');
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
            row.append('*');
        }

        System.out.println(row);
    }
}
```

Dry run for `n = 3`: the star counts are `1, 2, 3, 2, 1`, while the middle gaps are `4, 2, 0, 2, 4`. This gives the expected widening and narrowing shape.

The row uses a `StringBuilder`, which keeps printing simple and avoids many separate print calls.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the largest row stored in the builder.

Instead of separately printing the upper and lower halves, this code treats the output as a grid. Every row gets a star count, and a column prints `*` when it lies in the left or right star section.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern20(int n) {
        int totalRows = 2 * n - 1;
        int totalColumns = 2 * n;

        for (int row = 1; row <= totalRows; row++) {
            int stars = row <= n ? row : totalRows - row + 1;

            for (int column = 1; column <= totalColumns; column++) {
                if (column <= stars || column > totalColumns - stars) {
                    cout << "*";
                } else {
                    cout << " ";
                }
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

The important edge case is the center row. When `stars == n`, the left and right sections meet, so all `2 * n` positions become stars and no middle spaces are printed.

This grid check is a little less direct than printing three row sections, but it uses one rule for every cell and does not change any input value.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because each of the `2n - 1` rows checks `2n` columns.
Space complexity: `O(1)` excluding the printed pattern.

The butterfly shape has two matching parts. The top half grows from 1 star to n stars, and the bottom half shrinks back to 1 star. The gap between the two groups gets smaller until the middle row has no gap.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern20(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                cout << "*";
            }

            for (int col = 1; col <= 2 * (n - row); col++) {
                cout << " ";
            }

            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                cout << "*";
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }

        for (int row = n - 1; row >= 1; row--) {
            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                cout << "*";
            }

            for (int col = 1; col <= 2 * (n - row); col++) {
                cout << " ";
            }

            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                cout << "*";
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 1`, the two loops do not repeat the middle row, so the output is just `**`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the pattern has about `2n` rows and each row can have about `2n` positions.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output stream.

